You are implementing a small bugfix in the Hono repo at the current working directory. You own ONLY these two files: src/context.ts and src/context.test.ts. Do not modify any other files. Do NOT run git add/stage/commit; leave changes unstaged.

## Bug
`src/context.ts` lines 414-434 contain the `set res(_res: Response | undefined)` setter. Current code:

```ts
set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
  if (this.#res && _res) {
    _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
    for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
      if (k === 'content-type') {
        continue
      }
      if (k === 'set-cookie') {
        const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
        _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
        for (const cookie of cookies) {
          _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
        }
      } else {
        _res.headers.set(k, v)
      }
    }
  }
  this.#res = _res
  this.finalized = true
}
```

When middleware has already put a cookie on `c.res` and a handler returns a new `Response` that has its own `Set-Cookie` header, the setter deletes the new Response's cookies and only keeps the old ones. BOTH must survive after the fix.

Important runtime detail: in Node/undici, `Headers.entries()` yields ONE `'set-cookie'` entry PER cookie. So appending inside the loop would duplicate cookies. Capture `this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()` once and append the old cookies once after the loop, and skip `'set-cookie'` entirely in the loop (leave `_res`'s existing cookies untouched). Preserve the existing behavior for all other headers, including skipping `content-type`.

Suggested shape (adapt as you see fit, but keep it minimal and clear):

```ts
set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
  if (this.#res && _res) {
    const existingCookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
    _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
    for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
      if (k === 'content-type' || k === 'set-cookie') {
        continue
      }
      _res.headers.set(k, v)
    }
    for (const cookie of existingCookies) {
      _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
    }
  }
  this.#res = _res
  this.finalized = true
}
```

Note: `_res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)` preserves the new Response's own headers (including all Set-Cookie values), so do not delete them.

## Tests (src/context.test.ts)
1. Existing test at lines ~430-445, `'Should set cookie headers when re-assigning Response to \`c.res\`'`, currently asserts the BUGGY behavior: after assigning a new Response containing `foo3=bar3`, it expects `getSetCookie()` to equal only the old `[foo, foo2]` cookies. Update it so the new response's cookie also survives (expect all 3 present, order-insensitive is fine). Do not delete the test; correct it.
2. Add a NEW regression test that directly models the reported scenario: middleware already set a cookie on `c.res` (e.g. `c.res.headers.append('set-cookie', 'middleware=1; Path=/')`), then a handler returns a new `Response` with its own `Set-Cookie` (e.g. `new Response(null, { headers: { 'set-cookie': 'handler=1; Path=/' } })`) assigned via `c.res = newResponse`. Assert both cookies are present and there are exactly 2 (guard against duplicates). Follow the existing test style (`describe('Context')`, `it(...)`, `expect`).

## Verification
Run exactly:
`pnpm exec vp test --run src/context.test.ts`
Ensure it passes. If the suite fails, diagnose and fix within your two owned files.

## Report back
Return:
- The exact diff (`git diff -- src/context.ts src/context.test.ts`).
- The command output summary (pass/fail counts), including the specific test names that ran for the new/updated tests.
- Any residual risk or edge cases (e.g. `c.res = c.res`).
Do not claim success without showing the actual test command output.

